#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166558 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "aeb0ab38-6a30-586d-bcb4-8d3c6b8c9d9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8409" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} + y + 6 = y^2$. Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n1. $y=-3$\n2. $y=-2$\n3. $y=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'(t) = f(y)\n$$\n\nif there exists a constant $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(c)=0\n$$\n\nthen $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = y^2 - y - 6\n$$\n\nand we get that\n\n$$\nf(y) = y^2 - y - 6\n$$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y)$.\n\n- $y=-3$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-3) = (-3)^2 - (-3) - 6 = 6 \\neq 0$. $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n- $y = -2$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-2) = (-2)^2 - (-2) - 6 = 0$.$\\quad \\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- $y= -1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1) = (-1)^2 - (-1) - 6 = -4 \\neq 0$. $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166559 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "499fe0bc-77df-53df-8493-7d4c107031d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "ccdc8d9f-641a-593f-8999-ea02e14b3ed3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8410" :question/problem "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}= y^2+4y$." :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'(x) = f(y)\n$$\n\nif there exists a constant $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(c)=0\n$$\n\nthen $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(c)=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that\n\n$$\nf(y)= y^2+4y\n$$\n\nso we solve the equation\n\n$$\nf(c)=0\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(c) &= 0 \\\\\nc^{2} + 4c &= 0 \\\\\nc(c + 4) &= 0 \\\\\nc &= -4, 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_1(x)=-4$ and $y_2(x)=0$ are the equilibrium solutions of the given differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166560 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba6a29e6-4dab-5854-815c-fa26292c31d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "eaeec2ed-cb61-5887-94a8-b7ab30aa5d1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8411" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}= y^3-3y^2-6y+8$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_1(x)=4$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n2. The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, $y_1(x), y_2(x)$, and $y_3(x)$\n3. $y_1(x) + y_2(x)+y_3(x) =-3$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'(x) = f(y)\n$$\n\nif there exists a constant $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(c)=0\n$$\n\nthen $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that\n\n$$\nf(y)=y^3-3y^2-6y+8\n$$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have that $f(4)=4^3-3(4)^2-6(4)+8 = 0$.\n- Statement II is true. We know that $y=4$ is a solution to $f(y) = 0$, and so $(y-4)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y)$. Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-4)$ using synthetic division, we get\n$y^3$$y^2$$y^1$$y^0$$4$$1$$-3$$-6$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$4$$4$$-8$$1$$1$$-2$$0$\nTherefore,\n$f(y)|= (y - 4)(y^{2} + y - 2); = (y - 4)(y - 1)(y + 2)$\nHence, $f(y)$ has three distinct equilibrium solutions,\n$y_1(x) =4, \\quad y_2(x) = 1, \\quad y_3(x) = -2$.\n- Statement III is false. We have $y_1(x)+y_2(x) + y_3(x) = 4+1+(-2) = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cedd6ab1-7671-57fb-b70e-996d90c05c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165511} {:answer/id #uuid "ceed2781-70a9-54b2-b622-28c6aee27867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165512} {:answer/id #uuid "0086c7e7-1a85-56ef-899b-8e5a752d0b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165513} {:answer/id #uuid "11111cd8-4b0d-58b3-8c07-9d40d47fb18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165514} {:answer/id #uuid "31dd359e-b5b5-5b54-aaff-df55a14c35f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31dd359e-b5b5-5b54-aaff-df55a14c35f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165515} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d827717-c0df-59ff-b5c4-582a47736264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165510}] :question/id #uuid "73083626-cc38-5982-9c18-9459efa65b4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95572" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} - y^{2} = 2y + y^{3}$. Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n1. $y = 0$\n2. $y = 1$\n3. $y = 2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba6a29e6-4dab-5854-815c-fa26292c31d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d22ef741-508b-54c5-811e-43007fcdf7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165571} {:answer/id #uuid "09541b96-fc29-5e76-887f-36c95d134c00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165572} {:answer/id #uuid "15227065-3083-5026-93e5-afdd3a890e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165573} {:answer/id #uuid "580099cd-75db-59a5-a6fe-9b291d7d964a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165574} {:answer/id #uuid "7281a96e-024d-54b4-a3ab-238627351e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d22ef741-508b-54c5-811e-43007fcdf7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165571} :answer-field/id #uuid "a55d6467-1dae-5884-a30b-1d80ab5ac367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165570}] :question/id #uuid "ff7b1af7-af38-533c-a6d2-a2ca545a8e42" :question/math-academy-id "q-95733" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{3} - 7y + 6$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_{1}(x) = 1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n2. The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, $y_{1}(x), y_{2}(x)$, and $y_{3}(x)$\n3. $y_{1}(x) + y_{2}(x) + y_{3}(x) = 0$"}]}
